## std::set — Set (Ruby's Set) method surface.
##
## Zero-Rust-stdlib migration, Phase 2 (Option C). `class Set[T]` below
## carries BOTH the FFI alias decls (`Set_<m>` → `ruxen_set_<m>`) AND
## the TRUE surface return types delegated via the builtin-receiver
## bridge (`bridge_builtin_method`, reached for `Ty::Set(_)` because
## `builtin_bridge::is_delegated_head` now includes the Set head).
## `method_home_key` maps `Ty::Set(_)` → `"Set"`; element substitution
## into the declared `T` is done by `substitute_generics_in_return`.
##
## `Set` is NOT in type-scope (`resolve_type_expr` builds `Ty::Set(_)`
## from a hardcoded arm); the anchor branch creates a parent DefId for
## FFI bookkeeping without touching type-scope, so the hardcoded type
## resolver stays authoritative for the receiver TYPE while the class
## below homes the METHODS.
##
## ## ABI notes
##
## Every method's true surface type is ABI-identical to its C symbol's
## wire width: `RuxenSet*` / `RuxenVec*` (`to_a`) cross the C ABI as
## I64, `uint64_t` (len) as I64 (= `USize`), `int` bools as I8 (`Bool`).
## The parity guard `tests/runtime_abi_derivation.rs` pins the widths.
##
## No residuals — every Set method has a real C symbol and a statically
## substitutable return.
class Set[T]
  lib "../hash/runtime/hash.c"
    def self.new as "ruxen_set_new" -> Set[T]
    def self.with_capacity as "ruxen_set_with_capacity"(cap: Int) -> Set[T]
    def size as "ruxen_set_len" -> USize
    def empty? as "ruxen_set_is_empty" -> Bool
    def include? as "ruxen_set_contains"(item: T) -> Bool
    def to_a as "ruxen_set_iter" -> Array[&T]
    def union as "ruxen_set_union"(other: Set[T]) -> Set[T]
    def intersection as "ruxen_set_intersection"(other: Set[T]) -> Set[T]
    def difference as "ruxen_set_difference"(other: Set[T]) -> Set[T]
    def insert as "ruxen_set_insert"(item: T)
    def remove as "ruxen_set_remove"(item: T) -> Bool
    def clear as "ruxen_set_clear"
  end
end
